[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Styled Components: CSS-in-JS in React"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Styled Components is a popular library for writing CSS directly within your JavaScript (or TypeScript) code, a technique known as CSS-in-JS. It allows you to use component-level styling, making your CSS more maintainable, readable, and less prone to naming collisions. This tutorial will guide you through the basics and advanced features of Styled Components."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use Styled Components?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Component-level styling: Styles are scoped to individual components, preventing naming conflicts and making styling more predictable."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dynamic styling: Easily pass props to your styled components to dynamically change their appearance based on component state or props."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Automatic vendor prefixing: Styled Components automatically adds vendor prefixes for cross-browser compatibility."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Theming: Built-in support for theming, allowing you to easily switch between different visual styles."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Installation"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To install Styled Components, run the following command in your React project:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"npm install styled-components"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Usage"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's start with a simple example. Create a styled button component:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import styled from 'styled-components';

const StyledButton = styled.button`
  background-color: #4CAF50;
  border: none;
  color: white;
  padding: 15px 32px;
  text-align: center;
  text-decoration: none;
  display: inline-block;
  font-size: 16px;
  cursor: pointer;
`;

function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <StyledButton>Click me</StyledButton>
  );
}

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we import the `styled` function from the `styled-components` library. We then use `styled.button` to create a styled button component named `StyledButton`. The CSS rules are defined within the backticks (template literals).  Finally, we use the `StyledButton` component in our `MyComponent` function."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Dynamic Styles with Props"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Styled Components allows you to pass props to your styled components and use them to dynamically change the styles. Here's an example:"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import styled from 'styled-components';

const StyledButton = styled.button`
  background-color: ${props => props.primary ? '#007bff' : '#6c757d'};
  border: none;
  color: white;
  padding: 15px 32px;
  text-align: center;
  text-decoration: none;
  display: inline-block;
  font-size: 16px;
  cursor: pointer;

  &:hover {
    opacity: 0.8;
  }
`;

function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <div>
      <StyledButton primary>Primary Button</StyledButton>
      <StyledButton>Secondary Button</StyledButton>
    </div>
  );
}

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we pass a `primary` prop to the `StyledButton` component. The `background-color` is dynamically set based on the value of the `primary` prop.  We also added a hover effect using the `&:hover` selector."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Extending Styles"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can extend the styles of an existing styled component to create a new one. This is useful for creating variations of a component. Here's an example:"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"import styled from 'styled-components';

const StyledButton = styled.button`
  background-color: #4CAF50;
  border: none;
  color: white;
  padding: 15px 32px;
  text-align: center;
  text-decoration: none;
  display: inline-block;
  font-size: 16px;
  cursor: pointer;
`;

const DangerButton = styled(StyledButton)`
  background-color: #f44336;
`;

function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <div>
      <StyledButton>Click me</StyledButton>
      <DangerButton>Delete</DangerButton>
    </div>
  );
}

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we create a `DangerButton` component that extends the styles of the `StyledButton` component. We only override the `background-color` property, while inheriting all other styles from the base component."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Theming"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Styled Components provides built-in support for theming. You can define a theme object and use the `ThemeProvider` component to make it available to your styled components. Here's an example:"},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"import styled, { ThemeProvider } from 'styled-components';

const theme = {
  primaryColor: '#007bff',
  secondaryColor: '#6c757d',
};

const StyledButton = styled.button`
  background-color: ${props => props.theme.primaryColor};
  border: none;
  color: white;
  padding: 15px 32px;
  text-align: center;
  text-decoration: none;
  display: inline-block;
  font-size: 16px;
  cursor: pointer;
`;

function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <ThemeProvider theme={theme}>
      <StyledButton>Click me</StyledButton>
    </ThemeProvider>
  );
}

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we define a `theme` object with `primaryColor` and `secondaryColor` properties. We then wrap our `StyledButton` component with the `ThemeProvider` component and pass the `theme` object as a prop. Inside the `StyledButton` component, we can access the theme values using `props.theme.primaryColor`."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Global Styles"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Styled Components also allows you to define global styles that apply to the entire application. You can use the `createGlobalStyle` function to create a global style component. Here's an example:"},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"import { createGlobalStyle } from 'styled-components';

const GlobalStyle = createGlobalStyle`
  body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: sans-serif;
  }
`;

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <GlobalStyle />
      <h1>My App</h1>
      <p>Some content</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we create a `GlobalStyle` component that sets the `margin` and `font-family` properties of the `body` element. We then render the `GlobalStyle` component at the top level of our application to apply the global styles."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Using with TypeScript"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Styled Components works seamlessly with TypeScript. You can define interfaces for your props and theme to provide type safety. Here's an example:"},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"import styled, { ThemeProvider } from 'styled-components';

interface Theme {
  primaryColor: string;
  secondaryColor: string;
}

const theme: Theme = {
  primaryColor: '#007bff',
  secondaryColor: '#6c757d',
};

interface ButtonProps {
  primary?: boolean;
}

const StyledButton = styled.button<ButtonProps>`
  background-color: ${props => props.primary ? props.theme.primaryColor : props.theme.secondaryColor};
  border: none;
  color: white;
  padding: 15px 32px;
  text-align: center;
  text-decoration: none;
  display: inline-block;
  font-size: 16px;
  cursor: pointer;
`;

function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <ThemeProvider theme={theme}>
      <StyledButton primary>Click me</StyledButton>
    </ThemeProvider>
  );
}

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we define a `Theme` interface for our theme object and a `ButtonProps` interface for our button props. We then use these interfaces to type-check our styled components."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"Styled Components is a powerful and flexible library for writing CSS-in-JS in React. It provides component-level styling, dynamic styling, theming, and global styles, making it a great choice for modern React projects. This tutorial covered the basics and advanced features of Styled Components, giving you a solid foundation to start using it in your own projects."}]
